course: chemistry
lesson: 15
title: Acids in the kitchen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Suck a slice of lemon and your whole face screws up. That sharp, sour taste is the taste of an acid: lemons are full of citric acid.
explain: Acids are a big family of substances that taste sour and react with many things. Weak acids are all over the kitchen: vinegar on your chips, the tang of yoghurt, the sharpness of oranges, tomatoes and fizzy drinks.
explain: Squeeze lemon into warm milk and it suddenly forms lumps: the acid makes the milk's proteins clump together. Cheese-makers use exactly this trick to make soft cheeses like paneer.
explain: Strong acids are a different story. Your stomach makes a strong acid to digest food and kill germs, and a car battery holds a strong acid that can burn skin and eyes, which is why batteries carry warning signs and must never be opened.
fact: Sour tastes come from acids; lemons contain citric acid, and sour sweets are dusted with acid powder that stings the tongue.
fact: Acidic food, like tomato or lemon, makes a sore spot in the mouth sting.
fact: Vinegar is water with acetic acid in it, made by bacteria from wine or cider.
fact: Most germs cannot grow in acid, so pickles keep for months in vinegar.
fact: Fizzy drinks are slightly acidic, because carbon dioxide in water makes a weak acid, and some colas also have phosphoric acid added.
fact: Acid softens tooth enamel for a short time, so it is better to wait a while after a fizzy drink before brushing.
fact: The stomach makes a strong acid that helps digest food and kills germs; a layer of mucus protects the stomach wall.
fact: Heartburn is when some stomach acid comes up into the food pipe.
fact: Ants and nettles inject formic acid and other chemicals that irritate the nerves in your skin.
fact: Dock leaves on nettle stings help mostly because rubbing and coolness soothe; they are not a strong chemical cure.
fact: Acid makes milk proteins clump into lumps; cheese-makers use this to make soft cheese like paneer.
fact: Car batteries contain a strong acid that burns skin and eyes, so they must never be opened.
fact: Gases from factory and car smoke dissolve in raindrops and make acid rain, which damaged forests and stone buildings; cleaner factories have reduced it a lot.
fact: Bacteria in yoghurt turn milk sugar into lactic acid, which makes it thick and tangy.
fact: Unripe fruit is more acidic; as fruit ripens, sugar goes up and acid goes down.
exercise: Why does a lemon taste so sour? | It contains citric acid | It contains lots of salt | It is full of baking soda | It is mostly a bitter oil
exercise: What is vinegar? | Water with acetic acid in it | Salty water taken from the sea and boiled | Juice squeezed from lemons | Oil mixed with sugar
exercise: How does vinegar keep pickled onions from going bad? | Most germs cannot grow in acid | It freezes the onions solid in the jar | It covers them in a layer of fat | It makes the onions too salty to rot
exercise: Why do fizzy drinks taste a little sharp? | Carbon dioxide in water makes a weak acid | The tiny bubbles scratch the top of your tongue | They are full of tiny bits of lemon peel | The cold makes them taste sour
exercise: Why do dentists say to wait before brushing after a fizzy drink? | Acid softens enamel for a short time | Toothpaste reacts badly with the sugar left over | Brushing straight away makes the drink fizz again | The bubbles need time to leave your teeth
exercise: What is stomach acid for? | Digesting food and killing germs | Keeping the stomach warm at night | Making you feel hungry | Turning food into blood
exercise: Why doesn't your stomach digest itself? | A layer of mucus protects its wall | The acid is too weak to harm it | The stomach is made of bone | The acid only works at night
exercise: What is heartburn? | Stomach acid coming up into the food pipe | The heart getting too hot after running | A burn from hot food on the tongue | Acid leaking out of the heart into the chest
exercise: Why does a nettle sting burn? | It injects formic acid and other chemicals | Its leaves are very hot from lying in the sun all day | Its tiny hairs are made of glass | It sucks the water out of your skin
exercise: Why do dock leaves seem to help a nettle sting? | Rubbing and coolness soothe it | They contain a strong alkali cure | They pull the acid out of the skin | They freeze the nerves completely
exercise: What happens when you squeeze lemon into milk? | The proteins clump into lumps | The milk turns into water | The milk turns bright yellow | Nothing happens at all
exercise: Why do car batteries carry warning signs? | They contain a strong acid that burns | They are full of very hot steam | They explode if anyone touches them | They are made of poisonous gold
exercise: What causes acid rain? | Gases from smoke dissolving in raindrops | Lemons growing on the hills near the clouds | Salt rising from the sea | Lightning heating the rain
exercise: Why is plain yoghurt sour? | Bacteria turn milk sugar into lactic acid | Lemon juice is added to it in the factory | The milk was left out to rot | Vinegar is mixed in to keep it fresh
exercise: Why is an unripe fruit sour? | It has more acid and less sugar | It has not had enough rain to grow sweet | It is full of baking soda | It is still too cold inside
test: Sophie licks a sour sweet and it stings her tongue. Why? | It is dusted with acid powder | It has tiny bits of glass on it | It is far too cold for her tongue | It is covered in salt crystals
test: "stomach acid is strong, so why isn't there a hole in me?" What's the answer? | A layer of mucus protects the stomach wall | Stomach acid is actually just warm water | The acid is used up on food before it ever touches the wall | The stomach is lined with metal
test: Harry: "dock leaves are a proper chemical cure for nettles, they cancel the acid". What's true? | They help mostly by rubbing and cooling | Yes, they neutralise the acid | Yes, they pull the stinging hairs out | No, they make the sting much worse
test: If you left milk with a squeeze of lemon on a warm stove, what would you get? | Lumps that can make simple soft cheese | Butter floating on the top | Clear sweet lemonade | Milk that turns bright blue
test: "dad's car battery is dead, can i open it and see?" What should the answer be? | No, it holds strong acid that burns skin and eyes | Yes, it's only weak lemon acid | Yes, as long as you wear a thick winter coat and gloves | No, because the battery will get cold
test: Why are some old stone statues losing their faces? | Acid rain slowly ate away the stone | People kept rubbing them for luck | Birds pecked their faces off | The stone slowly melted away in the hot summers
test: Ava says all acids are dangerous and you should never eat one. What's true? | Many foods contain weak acids that are safe | She is right, all acids burn the skin | Only the acids in sweets are safe to eat | Acids are found only in factories
test: In a cartoon, a hero drinks car-battery acid "for energy". What's wrong with that? | Strong acid would badly burn the mouth and throat | Nothing, battery acid is just like a sharp lemonade | It would make him glow in the dark | It would only taste a little sour
test: Which tastes most sour? | An unripe green plum | A ripe, sweet yellow banana | A slice of white bread | A spoonful of plain rice
test: "why's my curry stinging the ulcer in my mouth? there's tomato in it". What's going on? | Acidic food like tomato makes a mouth sore sting | Tomatoes are full of hot chilli | The curry is too salty for the sore | Curry contains formic acid from the ants in the spices
test: Nan: "vinegar's made from wine". Teen: "lol no it's from lemons". Who's closer? | Nan: bacteria make vinegar from wine or cider | The teen: vinegar is lemon juice | Neither: vinegar comes from the sea | Both: it is lemon juice and wine mixed together
exam: Identify the substance principally responsible for the sour taste of the lemon. | Citric acid | Acetic acid | Lactic acid | Formic acid
exam: It is given that pickled vegetables remain edible for several months when stored in vinegar. Ascertain the principal reason. | The acidity prevents the growth of most germs | The vinegar removes all the water from the vegetables | The vinegar forms an airtight layer of fat | The vinegar maintains a very low temperature
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the protection of the stomach against its own acid? | The stomach wall is protected by a layer of mucus | The stomach acid is too weak to harm tissue | The stomach is lined with a thin layer of bone | The acid is produced exclusively during sleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning acid rain. | It forms when gases from smoke dissolve in raindrops | It is produced by citrus fruit grown near clouds | It grew greatly once factories became cleaner | It is caused by salt evaporating from the oceans
